<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN" "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
  <overview>
    <p><code>System.AccessToken</code> is the pipeline job's authentication token.
    Direct expansion into inline scripts, executable task inputs, or artifact
    publication inputs exposes it to receiving code. Mapping it into the
    environment of an executable step also grants that code access.
    Log masking does not restrict what a receiving process can do with it.</p>
    <p>This query covers direct case-insensitive macro expansion in YAML,
    script and command task inputs, execution-step environments, and publication
    paths. It does not prove that a file or artifact contains the token or that
    token use is malicious. Variable aliases, shell environment references, and
    cross-job flow are not evaluated. Known non-execution task authentication
    mappings, such as a Docker login, are not reported solely for containing
    the token. Unknown task input semantics are not inferred.</p>
  </overview>
  <recommendation>
    <p>Avoid interpolating the token into script text or command arguments.
    When authentication is necessary, limit its permissions and repository scope,
    isolate the trusted authentication operation from repository-controlled code,
    and avoid publishing credentials. Environment mapping is preferable to
    command-line interpolation but still grants the receiving script access;
    review whether that script needs the token.</p>
  </recommendation>
  <references>
    <li>Microsoft Learn: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/pipelines/build/variables#systemaccesstoken">System.AccessToken</a>.</li>
    <li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/522.html">CWE-522: Insufficiently Protected Credentials</a>.</li>
  </references>
</qhelp>
